course: dictionary
lesson: 7199
title: What is petrol?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Petrol is a liquid fuel made from oil. It is put into cars and other engines to make them go, so you can think of it as the drink that keeps a car running.
explain: The sentence «The price of petrol goes up and down with what happens around the world» tells you that people buy petrol and that its price can change. When it rises, families have less money for other things.
explain: The words around a word can also warn you. «Dad snaps "Phone off!" at the petrol pump» shows that petrol is dangerous near a phone, because it catches fire easily.
fact: Petrol is a liquid fuel made from crude oil.
fact: Petrol is put into cars and other engines to make them go.
fact: Burning petrol gives off gases that pollute the air.
fact: Petrol catches fire easily and its fumes are harmful, so keep phones and sparks away from the pump.
fact: Electric cars use no petrol.
exercise: What is petrol? | *A liquid fuel made from oil, put into cars to make them go | A type of rock found in hills | A hot gas that cools a room | A sweet drink for children
exercise: «The price of petrol goes up and down with what happens around the world.» What does this tell you about petrol? | *It is something people buy, and its price can change | It is a food that people eat | It never costs any money | It grows on trees
exercise: «Dad snaps "Phone off!" at the petrol pump.» Why might Dad say this? | Because petrol is a kind of phone | Because the pump needs a song to start | *Because petrol catches fire easily | Because phones make petrol taste bad
test: «Trouble there can make petrol dearer far away.» What does dearer mean here? | Closer to home | *More expensive | Cheaper | Better tasting
test: Which fact is true? | Electric cars burn oil in a tank | Electric cars run on petrol only | Electric cars are made of petrol | *Electric cars use no petrol
exam: Petrol comes from which of these? | Rocks | *Crude oil | Sugar | Snow
